00:00:04.728 --> 00:00:06.270
MIKE PROCOPIO: Well,
hello, everyone.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.760
And welcome to Google I/O 2022.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.140
My name is Mike Procopio.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.725
I'm a senior staff software
engineer here at Google.

00:00:12.725 --> 00:00:14.850
And today, it's my pleasure
to be speaking with you

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.250
about extending Google
Workspace with AppSheet's

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.552
no-code platform
and Apps Script.

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:23.260
Let's have a quick
look about where we're

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.120
going today with today's talk.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.430
We have five key pieces
that we'll touch on.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.910
First is AppSheet, that's
Google's no-code platform.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.160
Next is Google
Workspace, so properties

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.920
like Gmail, Calendar,
Drive, Docs, and Sheets.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.340
We have Google Workspace APIs,
the Application Programmer

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.350
Interfaces that power,
each one of these

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.000
and enable
third-party developers

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.520
to programmatically
interact with Workspace.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.000
We have Apps Script, which is
Google's low-code platform that

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.220
wraps those Google
Workspace APIs.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.830
And then finally, we have what
we are previewing here today,

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.130
which is AppSheet's
Apps Script Connector,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:00.490
an integration that connects
all of these together.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:03.150
Let's talk about
Google's AppSheet.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.910
I like to say that the
era of no-code is here.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.710
And this comes from
the perspective

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.010
of watching programming
languages evolve over

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:11.753
many years.

00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:13.920
The earliest programming
languages, as you may know,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.160
were very rudimentary,
very low level,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.720
and close to the processor.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.080
Over time, these languages
got higher and higher level--

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.960
two languages, for example,
like Java and C++--

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.950
and then even higher level than
that, for example scripting

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.790
languages.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.500
And if you take
this to the limit,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.510
you end up in a
place that basically

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:38.940
looks like specifying things
using definition and semantics,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.990
and not actual code.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.040
How can we do this?

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.910
Well, it turns out that
many business applications

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.860
have similar structure, right?

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.570
Apps are built on a data source.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.110
Users interact with them.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.120
And so it's possible to build
a great variety of apps using

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.820
this general definition
of what the app should do,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.830
as opposed to
actually writing code.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.470
So AppSheet is Google's
no-code platform.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.290
It's a fast way to build
apps and to automate work.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.330
It lets an app
creator build an app

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.340
without actually
writing code, using

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.920
the definition of what the
app should do as opposed

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.170
to actual code.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.410
Here's what that looks like
inside an actual editor.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.750
So this is the experience
of the app creator.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:23.890
And we are creating the app
using the app definition.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.390
So instead of
actually coding, we're

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.550
basically saying what
the app should do.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.530
So here in the middle, we're
building an automation,

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.770
and this is something that
will preview here shortly,

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.220
which says, when something
happens, do this other thing.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.840
In this case, the
other thing is we're

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.800
going to call an
Apps Script function.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.670
So that's one way
we can edit an app.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.160
Another way is we can build
on top of a data source

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.800
and map the columns
inside the data source

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.370
to fields that the user sees
inside the app, and so on.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.130
On the right-hand side,
we see a live preview

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.530
of what the app
actually looks like when

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.230
it's running, in this
case, the mobile version.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:03.310
So one key thing that a no-code
platform needs to do well,

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.700
and that AppSheet does well,
is to automate workflows.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.950
We sometimes refer
to this as bots.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.190
What this basically means is
creating-- using a definition,

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.630
not code--

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:16.780
a set of expectations that
when an event happens,

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.850
we can listen for it and then
trigger some other action.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.030
So for example,
concretely, when a user

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.670
creates a data row
inside the app,

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.950
then we can do this other
action-- for example,

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:33.660
sending an SMS text message
or an email for approval.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.890
Another key thing that a
no-code platform needs to do

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.840
is to integrate with a
wide variety of services.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.690
So for AppSheet, this means
inside Google, of course.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.390
But it also means
a great variety

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.830
of third-party services.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.140
Now, you can build
an AppSheet app

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.310
on top of a variety
of data sources,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.110
including Office 365,
Dropbox, and Salesforce.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.690
And then using Apps Script,
which we'll see here shortly,

00:03:56.690 --> 00:04:00.660
you can then call into an
equally wide range of services,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.080
including Google
and other services.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.740
So Google Workspace is
Google's productivity suite

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.660
that has more than
3 billion users,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.820
helping teams of all
sizes connect, create,

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:11.600
and collaborate.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.270
So these are the apps
that we know fairly well--

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:18.170
Gmail, Docs, Sheets,
Slides, Calendar, and so on.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:22.770
Behind each Google Workspace
app is also a mature API.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.370
If we look back, we can see
many of the Google Workspace

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.650
applications, like Docs,
Sheets, and Slides,

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:29.300
date back over 15 years.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.850
But what may be less clear
is that each one of those

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.120
has an API.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.140
This lets an application
developer automatically

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.550
integrate with the Google
Workspace property.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.760
So for example on
Drive, programmatically,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.400
using the API, you can
upload and download files.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.860
Or in Google Docs,
programmatically,

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.710
using the API,
application developer

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.290
can automatically add
text to a document

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:52.940
or edit text in a document.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.770
Same goes with
Sheets and Slides.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.800
For Calendar, you can create
a calendar appointment

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.530
automatically, and send it
out, and manage its responses,

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:01.590
and so on.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.860
So we've seen the
Google Workspace APIs.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.000
We also have Apps
Script at Google,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.420
which is Google's low-code
development environment.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.780
This lets an application
developer, among other things,

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.730
easily access the
Google Workspace

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.520
APIs from a high-level
perspective-- more

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:17.960
of a scripting language.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:22.400
As we will see, this is really
key for the no-code space

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.300
because if you can connect the
no-code app with Apps Script,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.910
you can greatly extend
the possibilities of what

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:30.260
the no-code app can do.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.190
So a quick recap here.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.130
Let's see, we have
four key pieces--

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.770
AppSheet, we've seen, is
Google's no-code platform;

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:40.190
Google Workspace-- that's
Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Docs,

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.640
Sheets, and Slide; the APIs
behind each of those Google

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.130
Workspace properties; and
then Apps Script, which

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.250
is Google's low-code platform
that wraps those Workspace

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.590
APIs, in addition to other
third-party services.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.740
That brings us to our
core idea here today.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:56.690
The AppSheet Apps
Script Connector.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.370
And basically,
what this means is,

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:01.070
from the context of AppSheet,
a no-code environment,

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:04.760
an app creator now has access
to all of the functionality

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.080
that Apps Script provides.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.380
But it's key to note that the
person building the AppSheet

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.410
app, the no-code environment,
isn't necessarily the person

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:13.370
who wrote the script.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.020
So there has to
be a sensible way

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.570
to expose the Apps
Script script information

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:19.190
inside the no-code environment.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.600
Here's what that looks like.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:24.620
On the right, you can see
that the user has chosen

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.770
a script available to them--
in this case, the application

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:28.940
author.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:32.840
We list out the parameters
that are required to pass

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.850
into that Apps Script function.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:39.470
And then we simply bind them
to the data source on which

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.580
the AppSheet app is built.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.630
So here are just a few use cases
for how this might be useful.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.390
The use case that we'll focus
on today, including in the demo,

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:50.000
is to be able to create
an audit log by appending

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.120
a summary of changes that
happen inside the AppSheet app

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.490
to a separate data
source logging it

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.650
in a separate isolated
way-- in this case,

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.740
for demonstration purposes,
simply a Google Doc.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:02.180
There are other
use cases, though.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:03.800
For example, inside
Google Workspace

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.540
you can automate
Slides, for example,

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.340
adding a new slide every time a
personnel directory is updated,

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:13.610
creating and editing calendar
appointments, managing files

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.130
inside Google Drive,
or, equivalently,

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:18.380
calling an external service
to do something similar

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.140
with a different provider.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.750
OK, so in this screenshot,
we see an actual picture

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:26.000
of the Apps Script Editor
creating a function

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.790
that ultimately, we will call
from a no-code application.

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:30.473
As noted before,
the person who works

00:07:30.473 --> 00:07:32.390
in this environment,
this low-code environment

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:34.700
inside Apps Script, may
not be the same person

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.620
who's creating the no-code app.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.300
In many cases, these
will be different.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.295
And the skill sets
will also be different.

00:07:41.295 --> 00:07:42.920
So let's take a look
at the actual code

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.037
that we'll see here in
the demonstration shortly.

00:07:45.037 --> 00:07:46.370
This is an Apps Script function.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.230
It's a working
piece of code that

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.530
shows what it looks like to
create a reference to a Google

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:54.770
Docs document, and then
take the parameters that

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:56.510
have been passed
into the function

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:58.490
and append them to the document.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:01.340
So here we're using the Google
Docs API to automatically

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.460
script the content
in the document using

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.190
Apps Script and Apps
Script function, which

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:11.330
has data and is called by
an AppSheet Automation.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.120
Let's take a look at what that
looks like from the perspective

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:14.930
of AppSheet.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:18.230
As we noted before, the person
who wrote the Apps Script code

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:20.000
function doesn't
necessarily have

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.100
to be, in many cases
who actually won't be,

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.450
the person creating
the no-code app.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.370
From the perspective
of the no-code app,

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.650
it's as simple as choosing
the Apps Script function

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:33.630
and then mapping in or binding
the values from the data

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.850
source on which the
AppSheet app is built

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.890
to the parameters
in the function

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.809
that we're calling
within Apps Script.

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:42.000
And this is what that looks
like, here on the right,

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.400
simply going down through
the list of the parameters

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.030
and mapping them one by one to
the columns and the data source

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.470
inside the no-code app.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.330
OK, it's demo time.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:55.050
We've seen AppSheet, Apps
Script, Google Workspace,

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:56.730
and the Google Workspace APIs.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.470
Here's a demo of a use
case that we saw before,

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:02.670
where we create an audit log
as a result of some trigger

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.020
inside the AppSheet app.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:08.227
We do this by calling
an Apps Script function.

00:09:08.227 --> 00:09:10.860
Hello, and welcome to the
demo for the Apps Script

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:12.270
Connector for AppSheet.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.850
In today's demo,
we'll see step-by-step

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.640
how to call an Apps Script
function from AppSheet.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.700
We'll see how to set this up
using an AppSheet Automation

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.940
and how to pass in parameters to
the Apps Script function using

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.560
the Expression Assistant.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:27.870
For this demo, we'll be
using the Travel Request

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:29.890
tool, an AppSheet Demo app.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.970
This app is used for managing
employee travel requests

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.710
inside an organization.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.660
Using the app, an employee
will create a travel request,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.970
which will then be sent
to a manager for approval.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.910
So the travel request
contains fields

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.880
such as flight information,
cost, business purpose,

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.500
and other details.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.810
In any no-code app,
there may be times

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.600
when an app needs additional
capabilities beyond what

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.520
a no-code platform can provide.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.190
The Apps Script Connector
that we're demoing here today

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.770
greatly extends the
abilities of AppSheet apps

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:02.160
by letting them access the power
of Apps Script and all the APIs

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.530
and services that
it integrates with.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.230
In this demo, we'll use the
App Script Connector to call

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.580
a function in Apps Script.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.830
This function calls
the Google Docs API

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:13.980
to append travel request
details to a Google Doc, which

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.110
creates a simple audit log.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.720
This function will be called
from an AppSheet Automation

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.490
that triggers
whenever a new travel

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:23.250
request is created in the app.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.950
In this demo, we have
three key players.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.920
First is the AppSheet Editor,
shown here on the left.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.770
This shows the app definition
for the Travel Request tool

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:35.640
and also a live preview of the
app on the right-hand side.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.490
Next we have the Apps Script
Development Environment,

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.230
shown in the upper
right, which is

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:42.810
where we'll be editing the
function that we're calling.

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:46.230
And finally in the bottom right,
we see the Google Doc itself,

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.970
the audit log,
which we will update

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.240
in real time via Apps Script
whenever we create a new travel

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.230
request in AppSheet.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:54.400
Let's get started.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.220
The Travel Request tool is
already built and shown here

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:58.870
in the AppSheet Editor.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:00.730
The first step in
calling Apps Script

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:03.400
is to create an automation
in the AppSheet app.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.130
To do this, we select the
Automation tab, and then

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:07.390
click New Bot.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.710
The bot will listen
for an event,

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:12.340
in this case, whenever
a new requests record is

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:13.990
created using the app.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:17.350
When that happens, the
automation will call a process,

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:23.340
and in that process will create
a special Call a Script task.

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.700
Now let's expand that task where
we can configure the details

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.320
for calling the script.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.150
To configure the
script, we'll first

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.010
choose the Apps Script Project.

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.362
We have this created already.

00:11:33.362 --> 00:11:35.820
It's the script shown in the
upper right in the Apps Script

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.230
Editor.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:39.330
This script has been
saved to Google Drive,

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:42.970
and so we can select that here.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:46.060
Once selected, a list of
available functions in the Apps

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:47.840
Script Project will appear.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.180
Choose the function to call.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.100
In this case, there's
only one function,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.930
called Log Travel
Request Update.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.450
We'll choose that one.

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.340
Once the function
is selected, a list

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:00.110
of parameters needed to
call it will be populated.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:01.990
So in this case,
that includes details

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:06.080
like request date, requester,
travel origin, and destination,

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:07.210
and so on.

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:09.490
You can see this matches
the expected function

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.530
signature shown in Apps Script.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:14.440
Next we'll use AppSheet's
built in Expression Assistant

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.810
to choose which values
in the underlying data

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.270
source to pass as the
function parameters.

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.400
Here, we'll simply
map each parameter

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.920
to a single column
in the data source.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:30.130
So that includes ID, date,
requester, origin, destination,

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:32.570
and purpose.

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:34.120
And that's it.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.730
Click Save, and now
let's test our work.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:38.620
To do that, we'll
add a new record

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.310
using the live
preview of the app

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:42.730
here on the right side
of the AppSheet editor.

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.940
We'll just create a sample
travel request, in this case,

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.575
flying from Denver to San Jose.

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:53.650
We'll add in the details,
such as departure and arrival

00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:56.020
dates and business purpose.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:59.080
Note that not all of these
will be part of the auto log.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:03.590
Now, let's save this record
and then sync the app.

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:06.100
This will trigger the
automation we created earlier,

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.530
calling the Apps Script
function using information

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.650
from the travel request
that we just added.

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:13.750
The function uses
the Google Docs API

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.120
to directly edit the
Google Docs file, which

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.800
is shown in the bottom right.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.410
This typically takes a
few seconds to execute,

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:24.100
which happens in the background
on AppSheet's servers.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:25.720
And there it is.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.430
The details of
the travel request

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.010
are added to the Google
Doc in real time.

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:32.470
From here, we could add
in additional parameters

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.150
to the Apps Script
function or even

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:37.690
extend it to use other APIs
or services available in Apps

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:40.750
Script, like YouTube,
BigQuery, Google Analytics,

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.860
or other external service.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.365
Well that concludes our demo.

00:13:45.365 --> 00:13:47.490
Thank you for watching our
demo for the Apps Script

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:48.690
Connector for AppSheet.

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.087
We can't wait to
see what you build.

00:13:52.087 --> 00:13:54.810
OK, so we've just seen the
demo of creating an audit

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:57.090
log using an Apps
Script function called

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:00.850
from an AppSheet no-code app
as a result of an automation.

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:03.540
Let's take a look at next steps.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:06.480
Great first step for beginning
to work with AppSheet

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.880
and no-code in general is
to join the AppSheet Creator

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:09.992
Community.

00:14:09.992 --> 00:14:11.700
And then once you've
done that and you've

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.740
created your first
AppSheet no-code app,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.593
you can try out the
Apps Script integration.

00:14:15.593 --> 00:14:17.010
And linked here
is the Help Center

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.960
and the docs and tutorials
that will help you get started.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:22.290
And then finally, we have
an excellent how-to video

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:24.270
that gives a different
take and a different use

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.640
case for how to
call an Apps Script

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:31.570
function from the context
of an AppSheet no-code app.

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:32.875
Well, thank you, everyone.

00:14:32.875 --> 00:14:34.750
On the right-hand side,
you'll see some links

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.340
to some additional resources.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:37.375
I'm Mike Procopio.

00:14:37.375 --> 00:14:39.250
It's been my pleasure
to speak with you today

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:41.470
about AppSheet and Apps
Script integration.

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:45.370
And I hope you have a great
rest of your Google I/O 2022.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:48.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

